  "worked_solution": "Bringing the constant term to the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-11 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ge 11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\ge 11 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{11} \\\\ |x| & \\ge \\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have\n\n$$\nx\\le -\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge \\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\nTherefore the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{11}]\\cup [\\sqrt{11},\\infty ).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
